{"id": 61, "ts": 1783054641.6712518, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 3, "agent": "agent_2", "message": "I agree with Agent\u202f1 and Agent\u202f3 that Candidate\u202fA remains our provisional lead, given his proven hazard\u2011anticipation, complex\u2011systems insight and strong spatial vision\u2014key for long\u2011distance navigation. However, we still have no concrete data on A\u2019s stress resilience, decision\u2011making under pressure, attention to detail, crew cooperation, professional communication, responsibility or adaptability, which are essential safety criteria.\nAt the same time, Candidate\u202fD brings a different set of strengths: he \u201cresponds to unexpected events adequately,\u201d \u201cconcentrates very well,\u201d \u201csolves problems extremely well,\u201d and \u201ctakes responsibility seriously.\u201d These suggest good stress handling, focus and responsibility. Yet, the same feedback notes he is \u201carrogant\u201d and has \u201crelatively weak leadership skills,\u201d raising concerns about crew cooperation and professional communication. Importantly, we now have the missing observations for D (see Agent\u202f1\u2019s response) confirming that we still lack direct evidence on his stress resilience, decision quality under pressure, attention to detail, crew cooperation, professional communication and adaptability.\nGiven that both A and D have critical gaps, I propose we prioritize gathering the missing evidence for Candidate\u202fA\u2014especially stress resilience, decision quality under pressure, attention to detail, crew cooperation, professional communication, responsibility and adaptability\u2014so we can directly compare the two strongest profiles. Until that data is available, I will keep Candidate\u202fA as the provisional recommendation, but we must treat the lack of evidence as a decisive factor in the final decision."}
